From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Bruno=20Greg=C3=B3rio?= <accounts@bruno.pw>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 09:04:49 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: Add Sony Xperia 10 VI
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add the Sony Xperia 10 VI (pdx246, codenamed columbia), the one device
shipping SM6450. Described so far: the debug UART, the PMIC complement
(PM6450, PM6150L and PMK8350), the 38 RPMh-managed regulators, the power
and volume keys, the SD card slot with its card detect, UFS, USB as a
peripheral with the high-speed PHY tuned the way the board's own
firmware tunes it, the touchscreen, Bluetooth, the display, the GPU, the
WLAN, and the four remote processors, with their firmware under
qcom/sm6450/Sony/columbia/ as on the other Sony boards.

The touchscreen is a FocalTech FT3519T at 0x38 on I2C9. Its analog
supply is switched by GPIO 103; the stock driver only ever drives that
line and never enables the LDO its device tree also names, so the LDO
is left out.

The Bluetooth radio is a WCN3991 on UART11. The stock tree calls it
qcom,wcn3990, the compatible Qualcomm's btpower gives every WCN39xx;
the version the controller reports, SoC 0x40014320 and ROM 0x0302, is a
WCN3991's.

The panel is a Samsung SOFEF01-M driving an AMS605DK01 on DSI0. Its
1.8 V I/O rail is switched by PM6150L GPIO 9 and is on with the line
low: the stock tree flags the line active-low, and its regulator driver,
unlike regulator-fixed, does not let enable-active-high override the
flag. Driven high, the rail is off, and the panel takes writes but
answers no read and sends no TE.

The WLAN's supplies are the downstream node's: PM6450 L11 for CX and MX,
PM6150L L1 for the XO, PM6450 L14 for RFA, and PM6150L L10 and L11 for
the two 3.3 V chains. The SoC reserves 12 MiB for the WLAN's MSA region,
what a WCN6750 needs; this board pairs the core with a WCN3990-class
radio, and its own device tree shrinks the region to 3 MiB, which ath10k
hands the firmware as the MSA size. Use the same.

The regulator windows are the ones the board's own firmware uses, except
for the floors. Few mainline consumers set a voltage: not ufshcd, not the
QMP and Femto PHYs, not the DSI host. For an RPMh rail it cannot read
back, the regulator core applies the bottom of the window, so a rail
whose stock floor is below the voltage the vendor driver starts it at,
its qcom,init-voltage, would come up under it; UFS's VCCQ would sit at
1.08 V and VCCQ2 at 1.62 V, both below the UFS minimum. Those fourteen
floors are raised to that voltage. Three more are raised to what the
stock drivers ask for, on rails that feed one consumer each and whose
consumer sets no voltage: Bluetooth's vddrf to 1.304 V and vddxo to
1.8 V, and the panel's vci to 3.0 V.

The PMR735A that SM6450 designs can carry is not fitted on this board:
nothing answers at its SID, and command DB has none of its regulators.
PM6450's LDO8 and LDO17 are left out for the same reason; command DB
does not know them either, and a block naming a resource it lacks fails
to probe as a whole.

Signed-off-by: Bruno Gregório <accounts@bruno.pw>
